            <div class="step5-reading">
                <h2 class="step-title"><?php echo $_GET['archetype'];?>'s Strengths</h2>
                <img class="img-intext" src="/offer/paa/img/mandala-2.png">
                <p><strong>They Can Charm The Birds From The Tree&rsquo;s</strong></p>
                <p>Member archetypes can charm the birds from the tree’s not with their words, but with their charm, charisma, and ability to listen and relate to all kinds of people.  Member's don’t charm people to get their own way though, they charm because they can’t help themselves and genuinely want to connect with others.</p>
                <p><strong>Has Strong Morals and Values</strong></p>
                <p>Members have strong morals and values that are also usually very balanced and unbiased.  Their values are typically based on practicality and realism.  They have a strong sense of fairness and right from wrong which helps them to continue their natural charm offensive!</p>
                <p>It’s natural and straightforward for a Member to figure out what a person wants or needs easily - which is also why they are usually so popular socially.</p>
                <p><strong>Loyal</strong></p>
                <p>Members are also very loyal to their friends, family, and acquaintances; it’s just that sometimes they don’t feel the same sense of loyalty from their friends that they would hope for.</p>
                
                <h2 class="step-title"><?php echo $_GET['archetype'];?>'s Weaknesses</h2>
                <p><strong>Can Feel Trapped In Committed Relationships</strong></p>
                <p>Members are the epitome of the social butterfly, fluttering around from here to there, delighting and charming whoever they stop by to meet. They even take time to invest in learning more about the people they meet, in a way that probably no other archetype can do. </p>
                <p>However, the need for social interaction never leaves the Member, so as they enter into committed relationships, they can experience trouble and disharmony as they want to be out socializing instead of staying in with their partner.  The Member’s partner perceives this need as a lack of commitment to the relationship.</p>
                <p><strong>Their Need For Social Interaction Can Be Challenging</strong></p>
                <p>As much as the Member needs social interaction, it can be a challenge too, mostly because they tend to form relationships and friendships quickly, which often peak too early and then fade away.  This has dire consequences on the Members’ self-esteem and ego. </p>
                <p><strong>Their sense Of Self Is Often Quite Low</strong></p>
                <p>Because of the fleeting relationships the Member often experiences, and also the problems their social needs bring to romantic relationships, the Member can often feel left out, rejected and alone which hurts the Member deeply.</p>
                <p>The number of bonds that the Member forms (which are more than most) mean that they feel rejected more often than most too.</p>
                <p>This cycle has dire consequences on the Member’s sense of self.</p>
                <p><strong>They Find It Difficult To Express Themselves</strong></p>
                <p>Members tend to feel as though it’s rude for them to talk too much about themselves, and they’d rather not anyway because it makes them too vulnerable and open for rejection.  It’s the same for any form of personal expression.</p>
                <p>Members’ might be the best at conversation and rapport building, but that’s because they are good at getting others to express themselves even though they find it difficult to do the same.</p>
